'''Porte de Versailles''' ({{IPA|fr|pɔʁt də vɛʁsɑj}}; {{literal translation}} 'Gate of Versailles') is a [[Metro station|station]] on [[Paris Métro Line 12|Line 12]] of the [[Paris Métro]], a stop on [[Île-de-France tramway lines 3a and 3b|tramway Line T3a]], as well as the southern terminus of [[Île-de-France tramway Line 2|tramway Line T2]] in the [[15th arrondissement of Paris|15th arrondissement]]. It is named after the ''Porte de Versailles'', a [[City gates of Paris|gate]] in the 19th-century [[Thiers wall]] of Paris, which led to the city of [[Versailles, Yvelines|Versailles]].

==History==
The station opened on 5 November 1910 as part of the original section of the [[Nord-Sud Company|Nord-Sud Company's]] line A between [[Porte de Versailles (Paris Métro)|Porte de Versailles]] and [[Notre-Dame-de-Lorette (Paris Métro)|Notre-Dame-de-Lorette]].

On 23 April 1930, a collision between two trains near the station resulted in the deaths of two people and 37 injured. A northbound train parked in front of a red signal between ''Porte de Versailles'' and [[Convention (Paris Métro)|Convention]] was hit in the rear by another train that had passed two stop signals at full speed.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=31 August 2000 |title=Une dizaine d'accidents en un siècle |url=https://www.leparisien.fr/faits-divers/une-dizaine-d-accidents-en-un-siecle-31-08-2000-2001598594.php |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230428195522/https://www.leparisien.fr/faits-divers/une-dizaine-d-accidents-en-un-siecle-31-08-2000-2001598594.php |archive-date=28 April 2023 |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=[[Le Parisien]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 November 2020 |title=A Paris, la ligne 12 du métro a 110 ans : ces deux accidents graves qui ont marqué son histoire |url=https://actu.fr/ile-de-france/paris_75056/a-paris-la-ligne-12-du-metro-a-110-ans-ces-deux-accidents-graves-qui-ont-marque-son-histoire_37338902.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220504173338/https://actu.fr/ile-de-france/paris_75056/a-paris-la-ligne-12-du-metro-a-110-ans-ces-deux-accidents-graves-qui-ont-marque-son-histoire_37338902.html |archive-date=4 May 2022 |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=actu.fr |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Les Actualités Françaises |date=1 January 1963 |others=Camera operated by Claude Becognee |title=Après l'accident de métro, Porte de Versailles |url=https://www.ina.fr/ina-eclaire-actu/video/afe04004511/apres-l-accident-de-metro-porte-de-versailles |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211127001507/https://www.ina.fr/ina-eclaire-actu/video/afe04004511/apres-l-accident-de-metro-porte-de-versailles |archive-date=27 November 2021 |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=ina.fr |language=fr}}</ref>
[[File:Porte de Versailles L12 3.jpg|left|thumb|Remnants of the original station to the north of current station, with the tiling on the vault still intact.]]
In anticipation of its extension south to [[Mairie d'Issy (Paris Métro)|Mairie d'Issy]], the station was relocated south of its original location on 31 December 1929. The old platforms were removed and additional sidings to store trains were installed in its place. The tiling on the vault are the only remaining remnants of the original station; it can still be seen on passing trains. The new station was operated by the Nord-Sud Company for a single day as it was absorbed by its competitor, the [[Compagnie du chemin de fer métropolitain de Paris]] (CMP) the next day. Hence, the station was decorated in the traditional Nord-Sud style. On 27 March 1931, line A was renamed line 12. On 24 March 1934, the line's extension to ''Mairie d'Issy'' opened, ending the station's role as the southern terminus of the line.

On 10 January 1963, a second collision occurred near the station, injuring around 40 people.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=31 August 2000 |title=Le métro parisien a frôlé la catastrophe |url=https://www.ladepeche.fr/article/2000/08/31/102611-le-metro-parisien-a-frole-la-catastrophe.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211122093006/https://www.ladepeche.fr/article/2000/08/31/102611-le-metro-parisien-a-frole-la-catastrophe.html |archive-date=22 November 2021 |access-date=23 June 2023 |website=[[La Dépêche du Midi]] |language=fr}}</ref>

As part of the "Un métro + beau" programme by the [[RATP Group|RATP]], the station's corridors were renovated and modernised on 18 December 2009.<ref>{{Cite web |title=SYMBIOZ - Le Renouveau du Métro |url=https://www.symbioz.net/index.php?id=42 |url-status= |access-date=23 June 2023 |website=www.symbioz.net |language=fr}}</ref>

Since 2016, the Vaugirard Workshops, used for the maintenance of trains on line 12 just north of the station, has been under renovation as part of memorandum of understanding between the RATP and the city of Paris. It includes the construction of a new equipment maintenance workshop (EMW), the restructuring of its train maintenance workshop (TMW), as well as the creation of new social and private housing in two phases on a 2.3-hectare site. The new mixed-used development due for completion by 2029.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 January 2021 |title=Restructuring the Vaugirard Workshops |url=https://www.ratp.fr/en/ateliersvaugirard |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230510084114/https://www.ratp.fr/en/ateliersvaugirard |archive-date=10 May 2023 |access-date=23 June 2023 |website=[[RATP Group|RATP]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=4 January 2019 |title=Paris : aux ateliers du métro, un nouveau quartier dans le quartier |url=https://www.leparisien.fr/paris-75/paris-aux-ateliers-du-metro-un-nouveau-quartier-dans-le-quartier-04-01-2019-7980960.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230623073432/https://www.leparisien.fr/paris-75/paris-aux-ateliers-du-metro-un-nouveau-quartier-dans-le-quartier-04-01-2019-7980960.php |archive-date=23 June 2023 |access-date=23 June 2023 |website=[[Le Parisien]] |language=fr}}</ref>

In 2019, the station was used by 5,923,995 passengers, making it the 65th busiest of the Métro network out of 302 stations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Trafic annuel entrant par station du réseau ferré 2019 |url=https://dataratp2.opendatasoft.com/explore/dataset/trafic-annuel-entrant-par-station-du-reseau-ferre-2019/table/?sort=-rang&refine.reseau=M%C3%A9tro |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220121165739/https://dataratp2.opendatasoft.com/explore/dataset/trafic-annuel-entrant-par-station-du-reseau-ferre-2019/table/?sort=-rang&refine.reseau=M%C3%A9tro |archive-date=21 January 2022 |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=dataratp2.opendatasoft.com |language=fr}}</ref>

In 2020, the station was used by 2,449,859 passengers amidst the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], making it the 89th busiest of the Métro network out of 304 stations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Trafic annuel entrant par station du réseau ferré 2020 |url=https://data.ratp.fr/explore/dataset/trafic-annuel-entrant-par-station-du-reseau-ferre-2020/table/?sort=-rang&&refine.reseau=M%C3%A9tro |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220121081214/https://data.ratp.fr/explore/dataset/trafic-annuel-entrant-par-station-du-reseau-ferre-2020/table/?sort=-rang&&refine.reseau=M%C3%A9tro |archive-date=21 January 2022 |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=data.ratp.fr |language=fr}}</ref>

In 2021, the station was used by 3,268,157 passengers, making it the 93rd busiest of the Métro network out of 304 stations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Trafic annuel entrant par station du réseau ferré 2021 |url=https://data.ratp.fr/explore/dataset/trafic-annuel-entrant-par-station-du-reseau-ferre-2021/table/?sort=-rang&refine.reseau=M%C3%A9tro |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=data.ratp.fr |language=fr}}</ref>

